---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StructureNext from "../../components/StructureNext.astro";
import LessonIllustration from "../../components/LessonIllustration.astro";
import MapStructureDemo from "../../components/MapStructureDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import { lwwMapTerm } from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<BaseLayout title="LWWMap" description="The hikers compare timestamps to choose an answer for each line in their trail report table." territory="Structures" sheetTitle="LWWMap">
  <article class="register-lesson">
    <header class="page-intro"><p class="territory-label">Structures</p><h1>LWWMap</h1><p class="structure-summary">Alice and Bob still have copies of the trail report table. They change the gate-status line while they are apart. Can Carol settle their disagreement without asking the ranger to number the notes?</p><LessonIllustration id="structures-lww-map" /></header>
    <StructureFacts structure="LWWMap" family="CRDT" model="Per-key last-writer-wins map" updates="Write or cross out a text entry" merge="Use the latest time; at equal time, keep the removal, then use the writer ID to break a tie between writes." delivery="Safe under repeated and out-of-order delivery" bestFor="Offline string settings with a trusted clock policy" cost="Time and writer for each answer or removal" />
    <section aria-labelledby="clock-title">
      <h2 id="clock-title">A time beside each line</h2>
      <p>
        For SharedMap, the hikers use the ranger's ledger number to choose the gate answer.
        In an <dfn><a href="/glossary/#lwwmap">LWWMap</a></dfn>, LWW stands
        for "last writer wins." Here, "last" means the greatest timestamp,
        not the last note delivered. Carol compares the times on the gate
        notes, then does the same for any other line in her table.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...lwwMapTerm} />
      <p>
        Before they exchange gate notes, each hiker has a different local
        view. Carol wrote the bridge report earlier, and all three hikers
        copied it before they separated. Each line records an answer, a
        time, and a writer:
      </p>
      <figure class="notebook-comparison" tabindex="0">
        <table aria-label="LWWMap notebook lines before gate notes are shared">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Notebook</th><th scope="col">gate-status</th><th scope="col">bridge-status</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Alice</th>
              <td><strong>Trail open</strong><br /><time datetime="11:40">11:40 a.m.</time> · Alice</td>
              <td><strong>Inspection due</strong><br /><time datetime="11:30">11:30 a.m.</time> · Carol</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Bob</th>
              <td><strong>Trail closed</strong><br /><time datetime="11:42">11:42 a.m.</time> · Bob</td>
              <td><strong>Inspection due</strong><br /><time datetime="11:30">11:30 a.m.</time> · Carol</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Carol</th>
              <td><strong>Report pending</strong><br /><time datetime="11:20">11:20 a.m.</time> · Carol</td>
              <td><strong>Inspection due</strong><br /><time datetime="11:30">11:30 a.m.</time> · Carol</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>
          Alice and Bob changed only the gate line. Carol still has the earlier
          gate report; the bridge line is the same in all three notebooks.
          <span class="notebook-scroll-hint">Scroll sideways to read every notebook.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The ranger accepts Bob's note first and Alice's second. For SharedMap,
        Carol records Alice's answer because the ranger numbered it last. For
        LWWMap, she records Bob's answer because 11:42 a.m. is later than
        11:40 a.m. After both notes arrive, all three hikers record
        "Trail closed" on the gate line and keep the bridge line unchanged.
        The ranger still delivers both notes, but his delivery order does not
        determine the LWWMap answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the ranger supplied his ledger numbers as timestamps, both rules
        would give the same answer. This table is a paper example; the demo
        starts with empty notebooks and races only the gate line. Watershed's
        runtime on each hiker's client starts with local wall-clock time and
        advances it past any time that client has already seen for that line.
      </p>
      <p>
        The demo uses the station to deliver notes, but LWWMap does not need
        the ranger to number them. The hikers can exchange notes directly or
        after a delay and still agree on the value for each line. SharedMap
        needs the ranger's accepted order before everyone can agree.
      </p>
      <p>
        If two notes have the same time, the hikers keep the crossed-out line
        instead of a new answer. If both contain answers, they compare writer IDs.
      </p>
    </section>
    <MapStructureDemo kind="lww-map" title="Compare times on gate-status notes" description="Alice and Bob change the gate-status line. Carol uses the time on each note, not the ranger's delivery order, to choose one answer." rule="Use the latest time; if tied, keep the removal, then compare writer IDs." raceLabel="Race the dated gate-status notes" />
    <section aria-labelledby="clock-risk-title">
      <h2 id="clock-risk-title">"Last" depends on the clock</h2>
      <p>
        If Bob's watch is fast, his earlier gate note can have a later time
        than Alice's newer note. All three hikers will record Bob's answer,
        but it will not be the last answer written in real time. Advancing a
        client's clock past times it has seen cannot fix a fast clock on
        another client. Use LWWMap only when the clock policy and the risk of
        losing a later human edit are acceptable. Each notebook must also
        keep a crossed-out line until it is safe to forget the removal.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section aria-labelledby="source-title">
      <h2 id="source-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Sources:</strong> Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/lww_map_kernel.gleam">LWWMap kernel</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Implementation:</strong> The demo calls Watershed through the Gleam toolkit
        for the three-client demo. Each client's runtime supplies wall-clock
        times and advances each line's clock past the times it has observed.
      </p>
    </section>
    <StructureNext />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
